{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 44, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "the social network, directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, stands as a defining film of the digital age. released in 2010, it chronicles the founding of facebook and the subsequent legal battles that engulfed its creator, mark zuckerberg. the movie is not just a biopic; it is a sharp, fast-paced examination of ambition, betrayal, and the isolating nature of success. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as zuckerberg, portraying him not as a villain but as a socially awkward genius driven by a desire for recognition and belonging. his dialogue is rapid-fire, filled with sorkin's signature wit and intellectual density, which keeps the audience engaged throughout the complex narrative structure. the film jumps between two main timelines: the creation of the website at harvard university and the depositions where former friends sue zuckerberg. this non-linear approach highlights the contrast between the idealistic beginnings and the bitter fallout. andrew garfield shines as eduardo saverin, the co-founder whose heartbreak forms the emotional core of the story. his betrayal by zuckerberg is handled with such nuance that it feels both personal and universal. trent reznor and atticus ross composed a haunting, electronic score that perfectly captures the cold, sterile atmosphere of coding late at night and the underlying tension of the relationships. visually, the film is sleek and dark, using cool tones to reflect the digital world being built on screen. fincher's direction is precise, ensuring that scenes of people typing on computers remain incredibly suspenseful. the movie questions whether one can be connected to thousands of people online while remaining utterly alone in real life. it remains a timeless classic because it predicted the complexities of our current social media landscape, showing how technology can amplify hu